# Memory System — Implementation Specification
*Version 1.0 — 2026-03-03*
*Status: Implemented and live on main OpenClaw instance*

---

## 1. Purpose

This document is a complete implementation specification for the OpenClaw team memory system. It is intended to allow the system to be reproduced from scratch on a new instance. All architectural decisions, file contents, schema definitions, configuration values, and integration steps are captured here.

---

## 2. Background & Design Rationale

### 2.1 Why not the original spec (Qdrant + Kùzu)?

The original `MEMORY_ARCHITECTURE_SPEC.md` (v0.3) proposed Qdrant (vector DB) + Kùzu (graph DB) + a dedicated embedding server. This was rejected for the following reasons:

- The embedding server assumption was wrong — the local llama.cpp instance runs in completion mode and cannot serve embeddings without a full restart with a different model loaded.
- At the corpus size in question (~240 KB, ~300 chunks), Qdrant and Kùzu provide no practical benefit over in-process alternatives.
- Both require Docker containers to operate, adding infrastructure to monitor and maintain.
- Kùzu's advantage (hybrid vector+graph queries in a single call) only materialises at >100K nodes.

### 2.2 What was chosen instead

A fully in-process Python stack with zero new infrastructure:

| Need | Solution |
|---|---|
| Semantic search | `fastembed` (ONNX, CPU) + FAISS flat index |
| Keyword search | SQLite FTS5 (built into Python's `sqlite3`) |
| Knowledge graph | SQLite node/edge tables |
| Entity extraction | Local Qwen model via OpenAI-compatible API |
| Optional reranking | Local Qwen model (same API) |
| File watching | `watchdog` library |

**Scale ceiling:** This design handles up to ~500K chunks (~500 MB corpus) before degradation. At that point, swap FAISS flat for FAISS IVF and SQLite for Qdrant — the `memory.py` API interface is unchanged; only internals change.

